@* The macOS port.
\label{macport}Everything so far describes the program as it was first written, for Windows and
Direct3D~11. The folder \texttt{mac} of the repository holds a second version of the same
program for macOS, written for Apple's graphics interface, \emph{Metal}. The rest of this
document presents that port with the same method as the first version. It has a second
purpose: to show, as concretely as possible, which parts of a graphics program depend on
the platform and which do not.

The port was written under three rules.

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Same program.} The window shows the same panels with the same controls, the same
defaults and the same materials (the folder \texttt{assets} is shared). A student who knows
one version can use the other without instructions.
\item \emph{Same structure.} Every file of \texttt{src} has a counterpart of the same name
in \texttt{mac/src}, with the same classes and functions, so that the two can be read side by
side. The shaders keep their names and their entry points.
\item \emph{Same mathematics.} Shapes, sampling, the bump normal and the lighting model are
computed by the same formulas in the same order. Section~\ref{compare} shows how this is
checked by a program that compiles the CPU half of \emph{both} versions and compares their
results.
\end{itemize}

@ What changes is the platform layer, which consists of four independent pieces:

\medskip
\begin{tabular}{lll}
 & \textbf{Windows} & \textbf{macOS} \\
vectors and matrices & DirectXMath & Apple's \texttt{simd} library \\
GPU interface & Direct3D 11 & Metal, through \emph{metal-cpp} \\
shading language & HLSL & Metal Shading Language (MSL) \\
window and events & Win32 & Cocoa (AppKit) and MetalKit \\
\end{tabular}
\medskip

Dear ImGui hides most of the last row, since it has back ends for both systems. Metal is an
Objective-C interface, but Apple publishes \emph{metal-cpp}, a header-only C++ wrapper in
which every Metal object is a C++ class (|MTL::Device|, |MTL::Texture|, \dots). With it, all
of the port except the file that creates the window is ordinary C++, and the code can be
compared line by line with the Direct3D version.

@ The files of the port are these. The middle column names the Windows file that each one
replaces.

\medskip
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\texttt{mac/src/VecMath.h} & --- & the parts of DirectXMath it needs \\
\texttt{mac/src/Mesh.h}, \texttt{.cpp} & \texttt{src/Mesh.*} & shapes; only the vector types differ \\
\texttt{mac/src/Material.h}, \texttt{.cpp} & \texttt{src/Material.*} & Metal textures \\
\texttt{mac/src/Inspector.h}, \texttt{.cpp} & \texttt{src/Inspector.*} & the probe; same formulas \\
\texttt{mac/shaders/pbr.metal} & \texttt{shaders/pbr.hlsl} & the surface shader in MSL \\
\texttt{mac/shaders/gizmo.metal} & \texttt{shaders/gizmo.hlsl} & the line shader in MSL \\
\texttt{mac/src/Renderer.h}, \texttt{.cpp} & \texttt{src/Renderer.*} & the Metal renderer \\
\texttt{mac/src/App.h}, \texttt{.cpp} & \texttt{src/App.*} & the user interface \\
\texttt{mac/src/main.mm} & \texttt{src/main.cpp} & window and frame loop (Objective-C++) \\
\texttt{mac/CMakeLists.txt} & \texttt{CMakeLists.txt} & the build description \\
\texttt{mac/compare/*} & --- & compares the two versions \\
\end{tabular}
\medskip

@ \emph{How to read this part.} Wherever a piece of the port is \emph{identical} to a piece
of the Windows version, the port's file refers to the module defined in the first part
instead of repeating it. The note under such a module then says that it is used in two
sections, one in each version, and the index of module names at the end lists all of them.
Because \texttt{lp.ps1 check} tangles both versions from this one document and compares
them with the repository, this sharing is not a claim that may become stale: if one copy
is changed and not the other, the check fails. Everything else in this part is new code,
and the commentary concentrates on how it differs from its Windows counterpart.
